    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. To go a bit deeper, we're seeing cases where the morphology is extremely irregular—almost like unusual growths or strange textures that don't align with typical benign nevi. In those instances where the physical appearance is particularly grotesque or anomalous, is there a specific way to document the 'unusualness' of the lesion to justify a higher level of specificity in the L-series codes? We want to avoid coding it as just 'unspecified' if the presentation is clearly abnormal.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For truly atypical and unusual presentations of skin lesions, the documentation is critical. Capture all relevant attributes such as description, size, color, texture, and any other pertinent findings. In ICAD-10, if you're dealing with an extremely irregular and unusual lesion, you might classify it under L98.9, 'Unspecified local infection of the skin and subcutaneous tissue,' if infection is suspected, or L98.6, 'Local inflammatory dermatoses of the skin and subcutaneous tissue,' if an inflammatory process seems to be the primary issue. Both codes are less specific than others in L-series but can be appropriate for extreme atypicality where a specific condition is not ruled out.\n\nFurthermore, include a comprehensive notes section detailing a description of the abnormality and clinical reasoning. In cases where there is still not enough data for a definitive diagnosis, considering L87.9, 'Other specified disorders of the skin and subcutaneous tissue,' may allow for non-specific categorization while also indicating that the rarity and uniqueness of the lesion don't fit into other categories. Always include a narrative linking the physical examination findings with the coding choice, always seeking the most precise code while maintaining transparency with the healthcare facility's coding auditors.",
      "source": "target",
